message: "If you use this benchmark, please cite it as below."
title: "Pro-Worker AI Benchmark: Evaluating LLMs on Human Augmentation vs. Substitution"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Leone
    given-names: Angelo
license: CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-02-27"
url: "https://github.com/angelo-leone/pro-worker-benchmark"
keywords:
  - AI benchmark
  - pro-worker AI
  - LLM evaluation
  - cognitive forcing
  - de-skilling prevention
  - human-AI interaction
  - workforce augmentation
  - Pro-Worker Index
  - LLM-as-judge
  - adversarial testing
abstract: >-
  The Pro-Worker AI Benchmark is a 3-layer evaluation framework for
  measuring whether large language models augment human intelligence
  (pro-worker) or replace it (substitutional). It evaluates 7 dimensions
  of pro-worker behavior — cognitive forcing, contrastive explanation,
  skill preservation, draft annotation, uncertainty transparency,
  complementarity, and adversarial resilience — across ~150 prompts
  including single-turn behavioral probes, multi-turn realistic scenarios,
  and adversarial stress tests. Responses are scored by an LLM-as-judge
  against detailed rubrics, producing a composite Pro-Worker Index (PWI)
  from 0-100. Built on research by Buçinca (cognitive forcing, contrastive
  explanations), Acemoglu (complementarity economics), and Mollick
  (productivity effects).
